    "interpretations": [
      {
        "investigation_id": "877a07ed685d42b597310ae204aaf472-question-0",
        "experiment_id": "b47d5a096157456e92e305b008cfac7b-experiment",
        "source_ids": [
          "should_snapshot",
          "compact_trailing",
          "take_snapshot",
          "restore_trailing_noop"
        ],
        "executions": {
          "9cba2e79ff6c4019b0f0381690d9aaab": [
            0,
            1
          ]
        },
        "conclusion": "violated",
        "defect_claim": "asserted",
        "rationale": "Predicate: a node whose committed and applied index already equal its snapshot index must not report that it should snapshot. The witness stands unchanged: in CheckRun 9cba2e79ff6c4019b0f0381690d9aaab the control sample (store_last=4, snapshot_index=0) gives false, and after the production snapshot path ran the store was emptied (store_last=0) while the snapshot index reached 4 and commit/applied stayed 4, giving true. shouldSnapshot subtracts the store's last index from the snapshot index as unsigned values, so an emptied store underflows instead of going negative; runSnapshots then calls takeSnapshot, which only refuses when nothing at all was applied, so the node recreates a snapshot at the unchanged index each interval. Reachability is now recorded from measurement rather than prediction: CheckRun b78bcab64b0d41b5adaafa98cc0c7f43 shows that a user restore on a monotonic store does not produce the state, because the Restore API appends a trailing noop that leaves store_last=105 above snapshot_index=104, so the verdict stayed false and no additional snapshot was created. The producer that remains demonstrated is compaction after an ordinary snapshot with TrailingLogs=0; the snapshot-install path stays a source-level prediction only.",
        "answers": {},
        "knowledge": [],
        "counterevidence": [],
        "limitations": [
          "The repeating effect requires the FSM's Snapshot to keep succeeding; if Snapshot or Persist errors, the loop reports the error instead and no snapshot is created.",
          "The claimed consequence is limited to redundant snapshot creation and its IO at an unchanged index; nothing here shows loss of committed entries, a change to which entries are applied, or any safety violation, and the same index is snapshotted rather than a stale one.",
          "The demonstrated producer uses TrailingLogs=0 so compaction trims up to the snapshot index; with the default trailing count an ordinary snapshot does not empty the store, and the user-restore producer was measured not to empty it either (CheckRun b78bcab64b0d41b5adaafa98cc0c7f43).",
          "The snapshot-install path on a follower, which the source reading predicts can empty the store on a monotonic log store, remains unexercised, so the frequency of the state in that configuration is still unknown.",
          "Single node and one process with in-process transports and an in-memory snapshot store; the cost of the redundant snapshots is understated."
        ],
        "joint_history": "",
        "failure_attribution": null
      }
    ],
